// `Table(n)` bound to a `let`, through the shapes the row count has to survive.
//
// This does NOT guard the bug it was written for. Whole-program lowering
// inlines a binding by cloning it, and the clone rebuilt a Construct from its
// COLUMNS -- which a row-count frame has none of, keeping its size in
// `row_count` instead -- so the clone was an empty frame. Parity cannot see
// that: the reference side here runs `parser::lower` too, so both engines lost
// the rows together and agreed on zero. `Lower keeps a row-count frame's size
// when a let is inlined` in test_lower.cpp is what catches it.
//
// What this does cover is the emitter and the interpreter agreeing on these
// shapes once lowering is right.
let scaffold = Table(3)[update { k = 7 }];
scaffold;
scaffold[select { n = count(), total = sum(k) }];

// The same through a second update, where the row count has to survive the
// first one to reach the second.
let widened = Table(4)[update { a = 1 }][update { b = a + 1 }];
widened[select { rows = count(), sum_b = sum(b) }];

// A bare row-count frame keeps its rows with no columns at all.
let empty_frame = Table(2);
empty_frame;
